I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen framework’s analysis tools first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine them in GSAS-II, and validate that every submitted artifact comes from the final project.

The chemistry narrows the likely inventory to Sr–W–O products plus residual SrCO₃/WO₃, but I’m keeping the phase count open. I’m now mapping the peak positions and checking the frozen COD index before committing to any structural candidates.

The dominant phase is unambiguously scheelite SrWO₄: its diagnostic reflections at about 18.3, 28.0, 33.4, 45.5, 52.0, and 56.1° are all present. Several sizeable peaks remain unexplained, so I’m fitting the dominant phase first and will index the residual rather than forcing it into a known precursor.

The residual resolves into two patterns: a low-level monoclinic WO₃ triplet near 23–25°, and a stronger sparse pattern near 29.75, 32.60, 44.66, 50.55, and 57.00°. I’m testing that second set against the Sr₃W₂O₉ framework with independent monoclinic cell refinement; its reference cell is offset enough that a direct database match would miss it.

The multi-start cell fits are still running. Early convergence is slow because the third phase overlaps both SrWO₄ and WO₃; I’m letting each start finish so the candidate is judged by the whole profile, not just its strongest line.

The residual search ranks monoclinic fergusonite-type SrWO₄ above carbonate, SrO, and all remaining WOₓ entries. I’m now comparing it head-to-head with Sr₃W₂O₉; both can explain the three strongest residual lines, but only one should account for the weaker reflections without inventing peaks.

The head-to-head fit slightly favors Sr₃W₂O₉ because it uniquely explains the strong 44.66° line. Fergusonite uniquely explains weaker lines near 48.6 and 61.2°, so I’m testing whether those warrant a fourth crystalline phase or are intensity/profile errors in the three-phase model.

The remaining 22.10, 35.3, and 42.2° peaks match the documented high-temperature R‑3m SrCO₃ polymorph, whose strongest line is hidden under the dominant SrWO₄ peak. Because the sample was fired at 900 °C, I’m testing that chemically plausible precursor/polymorph before adding any more exotic oxide.

The carbonate multi-start is taking longer than expected because its principal reflection is almost exactly coincident with scheelite SrWO₄. I’m retaining only solutions where the independent carbonate reflections improve together; a scale gained solely from the overlap would not be defensible.

The four-phase trial lowers Rwp to 12.69%, with positive scales for scheelite SrWO₄, WO₃, Sr₃W₂O₉, and fergusonite SrWO₄. I’m now rebuilding the profile terms with physically positive widths and phase-specific broadening; the exploratory fits used correlated width parameters that are unsuitable for the final project.

With positive starting widths, the four-phase model remains stable (Rwp 13.25% after rescaling), so the phase evidence is not an artifact of negative broadening. The slower phase-specific size/strain steps are now refining the visibly different peak widths.

The unconstrained minor-phase coordinate refinement is not trustworthy: it drives Sr/W sites by roughly 0.4–0.6 Å while barely changing Rwp. I’m rejecting those coordinate shifts and preserving the published heavy-atom geometry; only modest, well-determined adjustments will be allowed in the final model.

Preferred-orientation tests are near unity for scheelite and WO₃, changing Rwp by only hundredths of a percent. That rules out texture as the source of the large unexplained residuals in those phases; the remaining tests target the two minor tungstates.

The unexplained lines also resemble the Fd‑3m pyrochlore-type WO₃/WO₃·½H₂O pattern in the COD, especially at 15.0, 30.9, 38.2, 50.6, and 86.9°. That assignment is chemically questionable after a 900 °C firing, so I’m requiring a decisive whole-pattern improvement before considering it.

The pyrochlore WO₃ candidate collapses to essentially zero scale or unstable cells, so I’m rejecting it despite the superficial peak coincidences. A physically sized high‑temperature SrCO₃ cell gives a small but positive improvement; its evidence is real but weak.

A broader framework search changes the picture: an Sr-substituted Cu₃WO₆ Pa‑3 framework matches the residual substantially better than the fergusonite/Sr₃W₂O₉ references. Sr₃WO₆ is chemically plausible as an Sr-rich intermediate coexisting with WO₃, so I’m inspecting and testing that analogue-derived model directly.

The Pa‑3 model is not yet converging from smaller-cell starts; its scale collapses because the strongest calculated reflections begin outside the observed residual maxima. The starts near the powder-correlation optimum are still running and will determine whether the analogue is viable.

The viable Pa‑3 start refines to a positive scale but only Rwp 14.88%, worse than the two-reference monoclinic description. This suggests the residual has a related off-stoichiometric framework but lower symmetry or more than one crystalline component; I’m keeping the Pa‑3 assignment provisional.

The analogue screening indicates that no single library framework explains the residual. The physically plausible low-angle behavior is best represented by two off-stoichiometric families—Pa‑3 Sr₃WO₆ and tetragonal Sr₇W₈O₂₈.₈—without the spurious 19° peak produced by fergusonite/Sr₃W₂O₉. I’m testing that replacement as a combined model.

The first two phases are secure—scheelite SrWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃. I’m now checking whether the remaining reflections are best represented by known Sr–W–O reference structures or by a chemically plausible analogue-derived intermediate; that distinction controls whether a minor phase deserves a submitted CIF or should remain explicitly uncertain.

A five-phase trial lowers Rwp to 12.48%, but one added model is an Sr-substituted analogue rather than a directly reported Sr compound. I’m treating that as a diagnostic fit, not yet as identification: the improvement must survive phase-removal tests and match the generator’s physically allowed distortions before I submit it.

The low-angle intensity deficit is systematic: the 18° SrWO₄ line is too weak relative to its 28° line for the current flat-surface model. I’m fitting the explicitly permitted surface-roughness term now; this should also reveal whether the analogue phase was merely compensating for that specimen effect.

The roughness fit is taking longer because each seed requires a full derivative refinement over 8,282 points. It is still progressing; the key acceptance test is that the fitted A/B values remain inside the generator’s stated physical ranges and do not drive any phase scale negative.

The physical roughness model is accepted and cuts Rwp from 12.48% to about 10.9%. Cell and instrument refinements only improve another ~0.15%, so the remaining mismatch is predominantly structural/profile-specific, not a global calibration artifact. I’m continuing with phase-by-phase line broadening, bounded by the stated size/strain ranges.

The unexplained 22.10°, 42.17°, 44.65°, 57.00°, 61.27°, and ~67° lines form a coherent high-temperature rhombohedral SrCO₃ pattern, but its strongest reference line near 27° is suppressed. I’m testing the allowed March–Dollase texture orientations rather than accepting the carbonate on position matches alone.

The carbonate texture test gives only a small additional gain, so its identification remains tentative. I’ve broadened the search to 1,234 oxide frameworks with A₃BO₆, A₂BO₅, A₃B₂O₉ and related stoichiometries, substituting Sr/W on both cation orderings and optimizing lattice scale. This directly tests whether the fergusonite and cubic Sr₃WO₆ fits are proxies for a better analogue framework.

The broad search’s strongest chemically plausible new hits are Sr₃WO₅ (several frameworks) and Sr₂WO₅, while the existing Cu₃WO₆-derived Pa-3 model remains competitive. I’m now comparing their full GSAS-II profiles on the actual instrumental and specimen model; candidates with sub-0.8 Å contacts or charge/geometry failures have already been discarded.

The I4/mcm Sr₃WO₅ analogue explains the distinctive 48.62°, 53.44°, 61.27°, and 67.04° lines while avoiding the fergusonite model’s unsupported ~19.4° reflection. Its fit is statistically equivalent to fergusonite before detailed optimization, but its chemistry and unique-peak coverage are better, so I’m carrying Sr₃WO₅ forward and dropping fergusonite as a decoy.

The selected six-phase model is now internally coherent and all active scales are positive. Sequential metric refinement is lowering Rwp further (currently ~10.23%); importantly, the Sr₃W₂O₉ and Sr₃WO₅ cells move toward the observed 44.65°/67.04° lines without requiring nonphysical peak widths.

The unconstrained instrument step tried to make the Lorentzian width negative, so I rejected it and fixed X at the generator’s physical lower bound (0.1), retaining Rwp ≈10.19%. I’m now normalizing all active structures to the generator’s isotropic-U convention and selecting Uiso within 0.003–0.020 Å²; this is important because several raw COD files carried zero or anisotropic ADPs unlike the synthetic truth.

ADP normalization improves the physical model to Rwp 10.12%, with SrWO₄/WO₃ Uiso interior to the allowed range and the two broad minor oxides preferring the upper bound. I’m testing only the generator-permitted texture axes and ratios now; this is the last major intensity correction before assessing small coordinate perturbations.

I’ve completed the broad phase search and am now auditing the selected six-phase refinement for physically valid peak widths and stable phase parameters. Next I’m checking that the refinement replay, exported CIFs, fractions, and profile all agree exactly before packaging the final files.

The profile audit passed: all selected phase contributions remain positive and the fit improved to about 10.0% Rwp with physically bounded sizes and strains. One refinement flag was inadvertently left active, so I’m clearing that and using the generator’s documented perturbation limits to decide whether any atomic-coordinate adjustment is defensible rather than overfitting.

The first coordinate test supports a real, modest oxygen displacement in scheelite (0.052 Å) and improves the fit. A much larger WO₃ heavy-atom shift was rejected because it exceeded the synthetic experiment’s documented mismatch range—this is the kind of guardrail that keeps the structural models interpretable.

The coordinate pass accepted only two small, well-supported changes: scheelite oxygen and the variable Sr site in the Sr₃WO₅ analogue. Other unconstrained coordinate blocks tried to move by 0.17–4 Å, so they were rejected. I’m doing one final bounded line search (limited to a few hundredths of an ångström) to see whether those blocks contain any reproducible local signal without allowing chemically implausible motion.

The texture refinement found finite preferred-orientation corrections for WO₃ and the Sr₃WO₅ analogue, while the very weak carbonate and cubic Sr₃WO₆ terms remain boundary-limited and therefore less certain. I’m now testing only the three anisotropic microstrain axes that the measurement generator permits; any unphysical or unsupported anisotropy will be discarded.

The anisotropic-width test rejects anisotropy for WO₃ but finds a stable, in-range [100] anisotropy for Sr₃W₂O₉ (about 7,370 ppm transverse and 3,790 ppm axial), improving the whole-pattern fit. The remaining phases are still being tested against the same physical bounds.

The residual is not random: several underfit lines at 22.10°, 34.10°, 44.65°, 53.44°, 57.01°, and 67.03° form a coherent calcite-type SrCO₃ sequence. I’m checking all available SrCO₃ records and their oxygen-ordering variants, because the current carbonate identification is chemically plausible but its intensity model is clearly the least secure part of the fit.

The full analogue re-screen is now comparing the remaining residual against roughly 1,200 oxide frameworks after removing the six modeled contributions. This is a deliberately broad check for a missed Sr–W–O intermediate; it is also a useful test of whether the weak “carbonate” assignment is merely absorbing unrelated peaks.

The broad search found a substantially better explanation for the weak residual than carbonate: an I-42d Sr₂W₃O₈ framework adapted from the Co₃(AsO₄)₂ structural family. Replacing carbonate lowers Rwp from 9.74% to 9.46%, with a stable tetragonal cell (a ≈ 6.574 Å, c ≈ 18.048 Å). I’m completing the reversed-cation and alternative-framework controls before accepting it.

Both carbonate and a second W-rich analogue reduce separate residual features, but profile improvement alone is not enough. I’m now screening their local coordination: the provisional Cmc2₁ SrW₂O₅ mapping may place W on an oversized Bi site, while the reversed Sr₂WO₅ mapping is chemically more sensible. I’ll retain only frameworks with credible Sr–O/W–O distances, even if an unphysical atom swap fits slightly better.

A chemically credible monoclinic Sr₃WO₆ polymorph is now the leading residual phase. Its P2₁/c model refines to a ≈ 6.005, b ≈ 6.008, c ≈ 11.145 Å, β ≈ 97.92°, with realistic W–O/Sr–O contacts and Rwp ≈ 9.33% before final rescaling. This is stronger evidence than the dense I-42d decoy, which I am dropping.

The eight-phase combined model reaches 9.07% Rwp, with all minor scales positive (about 2.0–3.3 wt%). Before deciding whether those trace phases are genuinely distinct, I’m re-refining each lattice in the combined model. This checks whether residual peaks are simply being split between phases because earlier cells were refined against an incomplete inventory.

The combined lattice pass is stable: all accepted changes are below 0.11% in length, and carbonate’s cell change was rejected because it worsened the fit. Rwp is now 9.01%. I’m doing a final texture/profile audit only for the two newly accepted analogue phases, then I’ll freeze the inventory and build the clean replayable project.

The texture audit is still running because each axis requires a full atomic-profile regeneration; no new parameters are being added beyond one bounded March–Dollase ratio per candidate phase. The current accepted model remains safely saved at 9.01% Rwp.

The monoclinic Sr₃WO₆ phase shows a modest but reproducible [111] texture at the allowed upper ratio (1.4), lowering Rwp to 8.99%. The low-symmetry Sr₂WO₅ axis tests are computationally slower; I’m letting them finish because this is the last model-selection step before export.

The model is now frozen at eight active crystalline phases. The two new analogue phases both retain positive ~3 wt% contributions after joint cell and texture refinement; I’ll label their structures as provisional in the report. I’m now removing the inactive decoy phase, exporting CIFs directly from the final project, and validating a fresh GSAS-II replay.

Native replay passes with no profile-review flags, and all eight project fractions exactly match the exported inventory. The geometry screen flags the carbonate only for its expected half-occupied orientational disorder, but it also flags unusually short O···O contacts in the chosen WO₃ reference. I’m checking equivalent monoclinic WO₃ records so the final structure is not accepted on profile fit alone.

The first alternative-WO₃ control imported oxidation labels incorrectly into GSAS-II, so its apparent poor fit was not a valid comparison. I stopped that run and am normalizing the candidate CIFs to neutral element labels before repeating; the accepted final project itself is unaffected.

The normalized WO₃ controls confirm that the unusual P2₁/c reference is pattern-specific: a conventional physical P2₁/c WO₃ model gives 9.28% Rwp versus 8.96% for the selected model, while another polymorph is much worse. I’ll retain the pattern-supported reference but explicitly flag its short O···O geometry and structural uncertainty.

Analysis complete. The eight-phase model gives Rwp = 8.961%, Rp = 6.297%, and GOF = 2.538.

